+---
+id: dl-001-json-pointer-resolution-semantics-for-resolvepointer
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Pointer resolution semantics for resolvePointer"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+## Context
+
+Several upcoming features build on `resolvePointer`, and the library must follow RFC 6901 exactly.
+The RFC leaves some behaviour to the application (what a non-resolving pointer does).
+
+## Decision
+
+- Only the string form is accepted (`""` or `/`-prefixed); the URI fragment form (`#/...`) is invalid.
+- `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`; unescape `~1` first, then `~0`.
+- Array tokens must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*` and be in range; `-` (past-the-end) never resolves, so it throws.
+- Object members are looked up as own properties only, so `__proto__`, `toString`, `constructor` do not resolve.
+- Invalid pointers and non-resolving pointers both throw `Error`; a non-string pointer throws `TypeError`.
+- Present values, including `null`, `false` and `0`, are returned; descending into a scalar throws.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Later features (e.g. updates) can rely on a throw meaning "no such location" and on no prototype leakage.
+Callers needing a default must catch the error. The two failure kinds share the `Error` type.
